From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 22:04:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: vdpu383: warm the decoder up at power-up

Hardware H.264 decode on RK3576 is wrong for a whole power cycle at a
time, roughly one power-up in three. In every frame of an affected
session, rows r % 8 == 4 of every plane hold

    Clip1(q0 - Clip3(-tc, tc, ((q0 - p0) * 4 + (p1 - q1) + 4) >> 3))

with p0 = p1 = 0 -- H.264 8.7.2.3's normal-filter equation for q0, on a
horizontal edge the stream does not contain, with the p side reading
zero. tc is the only value that varies, one integer per power cycle,
and the values seen (37, 38, 40, 46) are all outside the legal range,
so it is not derived from the stream.

The state is latched when the decoder's power domain comes up. Within
one power cycle the same clip decoded before and after four other
decodes returns byte-identical output, tc is shared across resolutions
and transform sizes, and no bit of the 448 mapped registers predicts
the outcome either before or after a decode. Nothing programmed per run
moves it, and nothing precedes the first decode of a power cycle:
runtime resume enables the clocks and returns.

Decode a canned 2x2 H.264 IDR frame through the link-mode engine from
runtime resume, which is the one point that does precede every decode
of the cycle. The vendor driver carries an RK3576-only workaround for
this block that does the same from its own runtime resume, gated on the
RK3576 compatible alone. Build the job once at probe into a two-page
coherent buffer -- the register image and the link node that points at
it carry the buffer's own IOVA -- then at resume mask the block's
interrupt, program the link registers, kick, poll for completion and
put the link registers back. The job has no V4L2 context behind it, so
the host interrupt is disabled across it as well.

What matters is that the block completes a decode, and the job carries
only what that takes: the decode mode, a core timeout, the stream, and
the frame to write it into. Each piece was measured on an H96 MAX M9
against a control interleaved draw by draw, taking the power-up as the
trial, 400 power-ups per arm:

    arm                          corrupt   vs control
    control                      126/400   31.5%
    warm-up                        0/400   CMH p = 1e-33
    link engine enabled, no job  129/400   p = 0.82
    without the RCB pointers       0/400   CMH p = 4e-36
    without reg16 error-proc       0/400   CMH p = 4e-31
    without reg20/21 cabac masks   0/400   CMH p = 4e-31
    without reg13 core timeout   134/400   p = 0.22

So enabling the link engine without submitting anything is worth
nothing -- it moves neither the rate nor the distribution of latched
states behind it (chi-square p = 0.71) -- while the core timeout is
load-bearing: with it at zero the job still completes and reports
ready, but the block retires it without decoding and the fix is gone.
The RCB row caches, the error-processing word and the CABAC error masks
are all removable with no effect, so none of them is programmed.

Three further variants -- an empty register image, no stream, and no
output pointer -- leave a job the block never completes, and an
incomplete job leaves it unable to decode either codec for the rest of
the power cycle. That is why a warm-up timeout is warned about rather
than ignored: it means the power cycle is lost.

HEVC, which is bit-exact on this block in every arm of every campaign
run here, stays so throughout. No draw is mixed -- every corrupt
power-up is corrupt on every decode and every clean one clean on all of
them -- so the arms produce the latched defect or nothing, not garbage.
Every arm was checked to be decoding in hardware rather than falling
back to software, which would otherwise emit the reference hash and
read as a fix.

The job costs about 315 us on a resume that already takes about 31 ms.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu383-regs.h     |  31 +++
 .../media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c   | 187 +++++++++++++++++-
 .../media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.h   |  17 ++
 3 files changed, 234 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu383-regs.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu383-regs.h
index 2ecb953f8..c0f6007bb 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu383-regs.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu383-regs.h
@@ -29,6 +29,29 @@
 
 #define VDPU383_LINK_IP_ENABLE		0x58
 #define VDPU383_IP_CRU_MODE		BIT(24)
+/*
+ * Masks the block's interrupt output. Held set across the power-up warm-up job,
+ * so that a completion raised with no V4L2 job in flight cannot reach the
+ * driver's handler.
+ */
+#define VDPU383_IP_IRQ_DIS		BIT(15)
+
+/*
+ * Link-mode engine. The codec backends drive the block by writing its register
+ * file directly and kicking DEC_ENABLE; link mode instead takes a node
+ * descriptor in memory that points at three register segments, and is how the
+ * power-up warm-up job is submitted.
+ */
+#define VDPU383_LINK_CCU_MODE		0x00
+#define VDPU383_LINK_NODE_ADDR		0x04
+#define VDPU383_LINK_MODE		0x08
+#define VDPU383_LINK_CFG		0x0c
+#define VDPU383_LINK_EN			0x18
+#define VDPU383_CCU_MODE_VAL		0x00010001
+#define VDPU383_LINK_MODE_VAL		0x00000001
+#define VDPU383_LINK_EN_VAL		0x00000001
+#define VDPU383_LINK_CFG_VAL		0x00000001
+#define VDPU383_LINK_TIMEOUT_VAL	0x0007ffff
 
 #define VDPU383_LINK_DEC_ENABLE		0x40
 #define VDPU383_DEC_E_BIT		BIT(0)
@@ -41,6 +64,14 @@
 #define VDPU383_STA_INT_DEC_RDY_STA	BIT(0)
 #define VDPU383_STA_INT_SOFTRESET_RDY	(BIT(10) | BIT(11))
 #define VDPU383_STA_INT_ALL		0x3ff
+/* Every status bit but DEC_RDY_STA, i.e. the error flags. */
+#define VDPU383_STA_INT_ERR		0x3fe
+
+/*
+ * INT_EN and STA_INT are write-masked: the upper half enables the write, the
+ * lower half carries the value. This clears all sixteen.
+ */
+#define VDPU383_WM16_CLEAR_ALL		0xffff0000
 
 /*
  * Reference read caches, in the "cache" reg region. Three identical banks
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
index 5a4faf958..2f1e492d3 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
@@ -11,9 +11,12 @@
 
 #include <linux/hw_bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/genalloc.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/iommu.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
@@ -39,6 +42,41 @@ module_param(read_cache, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(read_cache,
 		 "Enable the VDPU383 reference read caches (default 1; 0 to measure against)");
 
+/*
+ * The canned power-up warm-up job, laid out in one two-page coherent buffer: a
+ * 32-byte H.264 IDR slice and the parameter set that describes it, the frame it
+ * decodes into and its colmv, and then the register image and the link node that
+ * points at it.
+ */
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_SIZE		(2 * PAGE_SIZE)
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_RLC		0
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_PPS		64
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_RPS		320
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_OUT		1344
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_COLMV		2880
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS		4096
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG0		(VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS + 32)
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG1		(VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS + 256)
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG2		(VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS + 512)
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_DEBUG		(VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS + 1024)
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_MAGIC		0x76543210
+#define VDPU383_WARMUP_TABLE_INFO	0x76543212
+
+/* A 2x2 IDR slice, and the parameter set that describes it. */
+static const u8 vdpu383_warmup_rlc[] = {
+	0x00, 0x00, 0x01, 0x65, 0x88, 0x82, 0x0b, 0x01,
+	0x2f, 0x08, 0xc5, 0x00, 0x01, 0x51, 0x78, 0xe0,
+	0x00, 0x24, 0xf7, 0x1c, 0x00, 0x04, 0xcc, 0xeb,
+	0x89, 0xd7, 0x80, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
+};
+
+static const u8 vdpu383_warmup_pps[] = {
+	0x40, 0x26, 0x00, 0x10, 0x04, 0x08, 0x00, 0x08,
+	0x80, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x40, 0x01, 0xd8,
+	0x07, 0x7c, 0x7a, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x04,
+	0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
+};
+
 static bool rkvdec_image_fmt_match(enum rkvdec_image_fmt fmt1,
 				   enum rkvdec_image_fmt fmt2)
 {
@@ -1176,6 +1214,130 @@ void rkvdec_vdpu383_config_read_cache(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec)
 	writel(VDPU383_CACHE_MAX_READS_VAL, rkvdec->cache + VDPU383_CACHE_MAX_READS);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Build the canned link-mode job once, at probe. The register image and the node
+ * that points at it carry the buffer's own IOVA, so neither can be built before
+ * the buffer exists.
+ */
+static int rkvdec_vdpu383_warmup_init(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec)
+{
+	dma_addr_t iova;
+	u32 *reg;
+
+	rkvdec->warmup_cpu = dmam_alloc_coherent(rkvdec->dev, VDPU383_WARMUP_SIZE,
+						 &rkvdec->warmup_dma, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!rkvdec->warmup_cpu)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(rkvdec->warmup_cpu, 0, VDPU383_WARMUP_SIZE);
+	iova = rkvdec->warmup_dma;
+
+	memcpy(rkvdec->warmup_cpu + VDPU383_WARMUP_RLC, vdpu383_warmup_rlc,
+	       sizeof(vdpu383_warmup_rlc));
+	memcpy(rkvdec->warmup_cpu + VDPU383_WARMUP_PPS, vdpu383_warmup_pps,
+	       sizeof(vdpu383_warmup_pps));
+
+	/*
+	 * The register image, indexed as the block's own register file. Only what
+	 * the block needs to run the job to completion: the decode mode, a core
+	 * timeout, the stream and somewhere to put the result. A zero core timeout
+	 * retires the job without decoding anything, which reports success and
+	 * fixes nothing.
+	 */
+	reg = rkvdec->warmup_cpu + VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS;
+	reg[8]	 = VDPU383_MODE_H264;
+	reg[13]	 = 0x0000ffff;			/* core timeout */
+
+	reg[64]	 = VDPU383_WARMUP_MAGIC;
+	reg[65]	 = 0;				/* stream start bit */
+	reg[66]	 = 0x20;			/* stream_len */
+	reg[67]	 = 0xa8;			/* global_len */
+	reg[68]	 = 2;				/* hor_virstride */
+	reg[69]	 = 2;				/* ver_virstride */
+	reg[70]	 = 0x40;			/* y_virstride */
+
+	reg[128] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_RLC;
+	reg[129] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_RPS;
+	reg[131] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_PPS;
+	reg[168] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_OUT;
+	reg[216] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_COLMV;
+
+	/*
+	 * The link node header shares the register region's base and occupies the
+	 * six words below the first register segment.
+	 */
+	reg[0] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_OUT;
+	reg[1] = VDPU383_WARMUP_TABLE_INFO;
+	reg[2] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_DEBUG;
+	reg[3] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG0;
+	reg[4] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG1;
+	reg[5] = iova + VDPU383_WARMUP_SEG2;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Run the canned job through the link-mode engine.
+ *
+ * The block's interrupt is masked in hardware and the host interrupt is disabled
+ * across the submission, because this job has no V4L2 context behind it. The
+ * link registers are returned to zero afterwards so that a codec backend's own
+ * kick is unaffected.
+ *
+ * Silent when it works. The two warnings are the only signal that the block is
+ * running unwarmed and that H.264 output for the rest of this power cycle is
+ * suspect.
+ *
+ * Only safe with the clocks running, so callers must already have resumed.
+ */
+static void rkvdec_vdpu383_warmup_run(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec)
+{
+	u32 irq_val, status;
+	int ret;
+
+	disable_irq_nosync(rkvdec->irq);
+
+	writel(VDPU383_IP_IRQ_DIS, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_IP_ENABLE);
+
+	writel(VDPU383_LINK_TIMEOUT_VAL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_TIMEOUT_THRESHOLD);
+	writel(VDPU383_CCU_MODE_VAL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_CCU_MODE);
+	writel(rkvdec->warmup_dma + VDPU383_WARMUP_REGS,
+	       rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_NODE_ADDR);
+	writel(VDPU383_LINK_MODE_VAL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_MODE);
+	writel(VDPU383_LINK_EN_VAL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_EN);
+
+	wmb();	/* the node and its register image must land before the kick */
+	writel(VDPU383_LINK_CFG_VAL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_CFG);
+
+	/*
+	 * INT_EN is what the block raises on completion, so it must not be
+	 * cleared before the kick: doing so makes every warm-up report a timeout
+	 * while the job in fact ran.
+	 */
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout_atomic(rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_INT_EN,
+					irq_val, irq_val, 1, 500);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(rkvdec->dev, "vdpu383 warm-up job timed out\n");
+	} else {
+		status = readl(rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_STA_INT);
+		if (status & VDPU383_STA_INT_ERR)
+			dev_warn(rkvdec->dev,
+				 "vdpu383 warm-up job not ready, status 0x%08x\n",
+				 status);
+	}
+
+	writel(VDPU383_WM16_CLEAR_ALL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_INT_EN);
+	writel(VDPU383_WM16_CLEAR_ALL, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_STA_INT);
+
+	writel(0, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_CCU_MODE);
+	writel(0, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_MODE);
+	writel(0, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_EN);
+	writel(0, rkvdec->link + VDPU383_LINK_IP_ENABLE);
+	udelay(5);
+
+	enable_irq(rkvdec->irq);
+}
+
 void rkvdec_schedule_watchdog(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec, u32 timeout_threshold)
 {
 	/* Set watchdog at 2 times the hardware timeout threshold */
@@ -1772,6 +1934,7 @@ static const struct rkvdec_variant_ops vdpu383_variant_ops = {
 };
 
 static const struct rkvdec_variant vdpu383_variant = {
+	.has_vdpu383_link = true,
 	.coded_fmts = vdpu383_coded_fmts,
 	.num_coded_fmts = ARRAY_SIZE(vdpu383_coded_fmts),
 	.rcb_sizes = vdpu383_rcb_sizes,
@@ -1871,6 +2034,8 @@ static int rkvdec_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (irq <= 0)
 		return -ENXIO;
 
+	rkvdec->irq = irq;
+
 	ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(&pdev->dev, irq, NULL,
 					rkvdec_irq_handler, IRQF_ONESHOT,
 					dev_name(&pdev->dev), rkvdec);
@@ -1879,6 +2044,13 @@ static int rkvdec_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	if (rkvdec->variant->has_vdpu383_link) {
+		ret = rkvdec_vdpu383_warmup_init(rkvdec);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, ret,
+					     "Could not build the vdpu383 warm-up job\n");
+	}
+
 	rkvdec->sram_pool = of_gen_pool_get(pdev->dev.of_node, "sram", 0);
 	if (!rkvdec->sram_pool && rkvdec->variant->num_rcb_sizes > 0)
 		dev_info(&pdev->dev, "No sram node, RCB will be stored in RAM\n");
@@ -1931,8 +2103,21 @@ static void rkvdec_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 static int rkvdec_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret;
 
-	return clk_bulk_prepare_enable(rkvdec->num_clocks, rkvdec->clocks);
+	ret = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(rkvdec->num_clocks, rkvdec->clocks);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The power domain has just come up, and the erratum's state is latched
+	 * when it does, so this is the only point at which anything can precede
+	 * every decode of the power cycle.
+	 */
+	if (rkvdec->variant->has_vdpu383_link)
+		rkvdec_vdpu383_warmup_run(rkvdec);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int rkvdec_runtime_suspend(struct device *dev)
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.h
index 5f3918205..fd6285317 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.h
@@ -85,6 +85,12 @@ struct rkvdec_variant {
 	size_t num_rcb_sizes;
 	const struct rkvdec_variant_ops *ops;
 	bool has_single_reg_region;
+	/*
+	 * The block has the VDPU383 link window, and so both the erratum this
+	 * driver works around at power-up and the link-mode submission engine
+	 * the workaround is submitted through.
+	 */
+	bool has_vdpu383_link;
 	unsigned int quirks;
 };
 
@@ -146,6 +152,17 @@ struct rkvdec_dev {
 	struct gen_pool *sram_pool;
 	struct iommu_domain *iommu_domain;
 	struct iommu_domain *empty_domain;
+	/*
+	 * Kept so the power-up warm-up can silence the handler across a job that
+	 * has no V4L2 context behind it.
+	 */
+	int irq;
+	/*
+	 * The canned warm-up job, allocated once at probe on a block with
+	 * has_vdpu383_link and unused on every other.
+	 */
+	void *warmup_cpu;
+	dma_addr_t warmup_dma;
 	const struct rkvdec_variant *variant;
 };
 
-- 
2.43.0

